'''''Eriocapitella hupehensis''''', a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[Ranunculaceae]] family of plants and has an extensive [[Native plant|native]] distribution across a large area of [[Asia]].{{r|POWO}} The plant was previously known as ''Anemone hupehensis'' and is often still referred to as such. Together with several closely related species and hybrids between these species, in horticulture these plants are often referred to as [[Japanese anemone (disambiguation)|Japanese anemone]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Japanese anemones |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/anemone/japanese |access-date=2025-01-08 |website=www.rhs.org.uk |language=en-gb}}</ref>

The [[specific epithet]] ''hupehensis'', which means "from Hupeh" (i.e. [[Hubei]]),{{sfnp|Gledhill|2008|p=205}} refers to a province in China where the species is known to occur. In [[Chinese language|Chinese]], it is called ''dǎpòwǎnhuāhuā'' ({{lang|zh|打破碗花花}}),{{r|FOC}}<ref>{{cite book |editor-last=DeFrancis |editor-first=John |editor-link=John DeFrancis |date=2023 |title=ABC Chinese–English Comprehensive Dictionary |title-link=ABC Chinese–English Dictionary |edition=Pocket |entry=Dǎpòwǎnhuāhuā 打破碗花花 |publisher=[[University of Hawaiʻi Press]]|page=[https://books.google.com/books?id=xrOTEAAAQBAJ&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&lpg=PP1&pg=PA116#v=onepage&q&f=false 116] |isbn=9780824875855}}</ref> which means "broken bowl flower".{{r|Yandex}}

== Description ==

''Eriocapitella hupehensis'' is a [[Perennial plant|perennial]] [[herbaceous plant]] with a [[rhizome]]-like root structure. It is a clump-forming plant with 3&ndash;5 [[basal leaf|basal leaves]], each with a [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]] {{cvt|5|to|35|cm|in|0}} long. The [[Leaf#Morphology|leaf blade]]s are [[Glossary_of_leaf_morphology#Leaf_structure|ternate]] with a central leaflet {{cvt|4|to|10|cm|in|1}} long and {{cvt|3|to|10|cm|in|1}} wide. The lateral leaflets are similar to but smaller than the central leaflet. The stem is {{cvt|30|to|100|cm|in|0}} long, occasionally up to {{cvt|120|cm|in|0}} long. A [[Whorl (botany)|whorl]] of 3&nbsp;leaves (technically [[bract]]s) wraps around the stem. The stem leaves are similar in appearance to the basal leaves but somewhat smaller. The [[inflorescence]] is a [[Inflorescence#Determinate_or_cymose|cyme]] with 2 or 3&nbsp;branches and a primary flower stalk {{cvt|3|to|10|cm|in|1}} long. Each [[flower]] is approximately {{cvt|5|cm|in|1}} across. In its native habitat, the flower usually has 5&nbsp;[[sepal]]s (no [[petal]]s) but cultivated plants have [[double flower]]s with around 20&nbsp;sepals. The sepals may be purple, purple-red, pink or white. In the center of the flower, there are more than 100&nbsp;[[Gynoecium#Pistil|pistil]]s each 1.5 mm long, surrounded by prominent yellow [[stamen]]s approximately {{cvt|5|mm|in|1}} long. The [[fruit]]s are small ovoid [[achene]]s with straight [[Stigma_(botany)#Style|styles]].{{r|FOC}}

== Taxonomy ==
''Eriocapitella hupehensis'' was described by [[Maarten J. M. Christenhusz]] and [[James W. Byng]] in 2018.{{r|IPNI}} Like other members of genus ''Eriocapitella'', ''E.&nbsp;hupehensis'' was formerly a member of genus ''[[Anemone]]''. The [[basionym]] ''Anemone japonica'' var.&nbsp;''hupehensis'' <small>Lemoine</small> was described in 1908.{{r|POWO:60450400-2}}

== Distribution ==

''Eriocapitella hupehensis'' is [[Native plant|native]] to [[Asia]], in the [[Eastern Himalaya]] region, [[East Asia]], and [[Southeast Asia]].{{r|FOC}}

* Eastern Himalaya: [[Nepal]], [[Assam]] (northeast India), [[Tibet]]
* East and Southeast Asia: [[China]], [[Taiwan]], [[Myanmar]]
** [[Northwest China]]: [[Shaanxi]]
** [[Central China]]: [[Hubei]]
** [[East China]]: [[Jiangxi]], [[Zhejiang]]
** [[South China]]: [[Guangdong]], [[Guangxi]]
** [[Southwest China]]: [[Guizhou]], [[Sichuan]], [[Yunnan]]

The species was introduced into [[Czechoslovakia]], [[Ecuador]], and [[Germany]].{{r|POWO}}

==Ecology==

''Eriocapitella hupehensis'' along with four other [[taxa]] ([[Eriocapitella × hybrida|''E.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''hybrida'']], [[Eriocapitella japonica|''E.&nbsp;japonica'']], [[Eriocapitella tomentosa|''E.&nbsp;tomentosa'']], and [[Eriocapitella vitifolia |''E. vitifolia'']]) are known as [[fall-blooming anemone]]s.{{sfnp|Rudy|2004}} In its native habitat, ''E.&nbsp;hupehensis'' flowers from July to October.{{r|FOC}}

== Cultivation ==

''Eriocapitella hupehensis'' and its [[cultivar]]s are cultivated worldwide, especially in [[Asia]], [[Europe]], and [[South America]], where naturalized populations are known to exist. In China, ''E.&nbsp;hupehensis'' has been cultivated since at least the 17th century, probably dating back to the [[Tang dynasty]] (618–907). Hundreds of years ago, a semi-double form of ''E.&nbsp;hupehensis'' [[escaped plant|escaped cultivation]] and spread across China to Japan and Korea. This descendant of ''E.&nbsp;hupehensis'', now known as [[Eriocapitella japonica|''E.&nbsp;japonica'']], is a parent of the artificial hybrid [[Eriocapitella × hybrida|''E.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''hybrida'']].{{sfnp|Herman|2004}}{{sfnp|Rudy|2004}}{{r|Klingaman2006}}

